NORTHSTAR COMMERCE PLATFORM ARCHITECTURE DOCUMENT

Purpose and scope
Northstar Commerce operates a marketplace that lets independent merchants sell physical goods to customers in North America and Europe. The platform supports catalog management, price changes, search, checkout, payment authorization, fulfillment, returns, and merchant reporting. The company expects seasonal demand to reach twelve thousand checkout attempts per minute. The primary objectives are resilient checkout, predictable merchant operations, protection of customer data, and the ability for small product teams to release services independently.

System boundaries
The customer web application and mobile clients use a public API gateway. The gateway validates OAuth access tokens, applies request limits, adds a correlation identifier, and routes calls to backend services. Static content is served through a content delivery network. The gateway does not contain business rules; it exists to provide a stable external interface and centralized transport controls. Internal services communicate through authenticated HTTP for request-response work and through an event bus for work that may complete asynchronously.

Core services
The Catalog service owns product descriptions, merchant offers, availability summaries, and media references. It stores transactional catalog records in PostgreSQL and publishes CatalogChanged events after committed changes. The Pricing service owns price rules, promotions, tax category assignments, and the calculated price quote used at checkout. A quote has a short expiration and includes a version so Checkout can detect a price change. The Search service consumes catalog events, builds an OpenSearch index, and provides keyword and filter queries. Search is deliberately eventually consistent; a newly created product may take several seconds to appear.

The Cart service stores active shopping carts in DynamoDB because cart access is high volume, key oriented, and tolerant of eventual consistency for noncritical display fields. Cart writes use conditional expressions to prevent lost updates. The Checkout service coordinates the transition from cart to order. It is the only service allowed to create an order. Checkout first validates inventory and price quotes, then creates an order in PostgreSQL with status PENDING_PAYMENT. It sends an outbox event in the same database transaction. An outbox publisher later emits OrderCreated to the event bus. This pattern prevents an order from being committed without a corresponding event when a process fails between database work and message publication.

Payment and fulfillment
The Payment service integrates with an external payment processor. It receives OrderCreated events and authorizes payment with an idempotency key derived from the order identifier. Payment stores the processor reference, authorization result, and retry state in PostgreSQL. It emits PaymentAuthorized or PaymentDeclined only after its own database transaction commits. The Fulfillment service receives PaymentAuthorized, reserves inventory through warehouse adapters, and creates shipment tasks. Warehouse systems are older systems with variable latency, so the fulfillment workflow has retries with exponential backoff and a manual exception queue. A reservation expiry timer releases inventory if fulfillment does not progress.

Orders are not synchronously rolled back after payment authorization. Instead, the system uses compensating actions. If inventory cannot be reserved, Fulfillment emits InventoryReservationFailed. Payment then submits a payment void or refund according to whether settlement occurred, while Order marks the order as CANCELLED. This saga avoids distributed transactions across payment processors, warehouses, and service databases. Each event handler is idempotent: it records the event identifier and ignores duplicates. The event bus provides at-least-once delivery, so duplicate processing is an expected condition rather than an exceptional one.

Data ownership and consistency
Every service owns its database and exposes data through APIs or published events. Services must not query another service database. The reporting warehouse receives immutable business events through a streaming pipeline and therefore can lag operational systems. Customer service agents view an Order Read Model that joins events from orders, payments, and fulfillment. That read model is refreshed asynchronously and prominently displays its last updated time. The authoritative order state remains in the Order database.

PostgreSQL is used for data requiring relational constraints, transactions, and audited changes. DynamoDB is used for carts, session preferences, and rate-limit counters. Object storage retains product images, invoice PDFs, and raw event archives. Each database has encryption at rest, backups, and a documented recovery objective. Personal data is minimized in events: events contain opaque customer identifiers instead of names, email addresses, or payment details. A separate Customer Profile service handles personally identifiable information and restricts access by role.

Eventing and schema governance
The event bus separates domains into catalog, order, payment, fulfillment, and customer topics. Producers use a schema registry and add fields in backward-compatible ways. Consumers preserve unknown fields so that messages can flow during rolling deployments. Breaking schema changes require a new event type and a migration plan. Events include eventId, occurredAt, aggregateId, aggregateVersion, producer, and trace context. The aggregate version helps consumers recognize stale updates. Dead-letter queues hold messages that fail repeatedly, but an operational runbook requires teams to correct the cause and replay messages rather than silently discard them.

Deployment topology
Services run in multiple availability zones behind internal load balancers. Each service has a separate deployment pipeline, container image, health endpoint, and autoscaling policy. Production traffic is split gradually using a canary deployment. Database migrations run before a service version begins serving traffic and must remain compatible with the previous version until rollout completes. Feature flags separate deployment from activation. A regional disaster recovery plan restores critical services into a secondary region from replicated object storage and database backups. The team periodically tests restoration, including the ability to process delayed events without violating idempotency.

Observability and operations
Every inbound request receives a correlation identifier that flows into logs, metrics, and trace spans. Services record request rate, latency, error rate, queue age, retry count, database connection saturation, and business metrics such as payment decline rate. Alerting is based on user impact and service objectives. A checkout availability objective requires ninety-nine point nine percent successful checkout initiation over a rolling thirty-day period, excluding customer payment declines. Dashboards distinguish gateway failures, service failures, dependency failures, and business rejections. Operators can pause event consumers, replay a bounded event range, and inspect the state of a single order across services.

Security controls
Workload identities use short-lived credentials. The API gateway validates issuer, audience, and expiration claims. Service-to-service calls use mutual TLS and authorization policies that restrict methods by caller. Secrets are stored in a managed secrets system and rotated. Payment card data never enters Northstar services; browser and mobile clients use payment processor tokenization. Audit records capture merchant administrative actions, support-agent access to customer information, and privileged production changes. Sensitive fields are redacted in logs. Threat modeling focuses on account takeover, abusive automation, supplier feed tampering, and unauthorized access to order history.

Scalability risks and mitigations
The most significant risk is a flash sale causing a thundering herd of cart reads, price quote requests, and inventory validations. The gateway limits abusive traffic, but legitimate demand can still exceed downstream capacity. Catalog and Search scale horizontally, while Pricing uses cached rule snapshots and protects its database with connection limits. Checkout uses a bounded worker pool and sheds nonessential enrichment when latency rises. Inventory validation is intentionally kept synchronous because overselling is costly, creating a dependency on warehouse availability. The platform mitigates this with regional inventory replicas, short request deadlines, circuit breakers, and a queue for reservation reconciliation.

Another risk is event lag. During a consumer outage, the event backlog can grow faster than recovery throughput, which makes the Order Read Model stale and delays fulfillment. Consumer autoscaling uses queue age as well as message count. Replay tooling limits concurrency by aggregate key to preserve order. The outbox publisher is a critical bottleneck because every new order depends on it; it runs as a horizontally scaled service, records lag metrics, and alerts when unpublished rows exceed a small threshold. A third risk is hot partitions in DynamoDB when popular carts or campaigns share a poorly distributed key. Cart keys include customer identity, and campaign counters use sharded keys merged by a background job.

The external payment processor is a fourth scalability and availability risk. Payment authorization has strict timeouts, idempotency keys, retry rules, and a circuit breaker. A prolonged processor outage places new orders into PAYMENT_PENDING and communicates a clear status to customers rather than issuing duplicate charges. Teams regularly test that retries do not create more than one authorization. Finally, OpenSearch indexing can lag when product feeds surge. Search remains available from its previous index state, while merchant tooling exposes indexing lag and allows prioritized reindexing for urgent catalog corrections.

Architectural decisions
Northstar chose asynchronous events for cross-domain propagation because independent deployments and failure isolation are more valuable than immediate global consistency. It chose the transactional outbox because database writes and event publication must be coordinated without a distributed transaction. It chose a saga with compensation because payment, warehouse, and shipping systems cannot share a transaction manager. It chose DynamoDB for carts because predictable key-based scaling outweighs the cost of relational queries for short-lived cart data. It chose PostgreSQL for orders and payments because those domains require strong transactional integrity, constraints, and auditability. It chose eventual consistency for search and reporting because customers can tolerate a brief delay there, while checkout and inventory decisions require fresher state.

Operational constraints
Teams must keep request payloads small, set deadlines on all remote calls, and make all externally visible write operations idempotent. They must treat event handlers as retryable and safe for duplicate delivery. New dependencies need capacity tests, failure-mode tests, dashboards, and an owner. Releases during peak retail dates require an approved rollback plan. Any change to the event schema must be reviewed by both producing and consuming teams. These constraints preserve the platform's ability to scale while allowing teams to evolve services independently.
